## 1. Purpose & Scope

This report profiles all 24 in-scope GPPS policy tables as candidate sources for the
alert-to-policy linkage pipeline. Three questions drove the analysis:

1. **Does each table actually contain usable policy text?** — and if so, is that text substantial
   enough to embed meaningfully, or too short to carry signal?
2. **What linkage exists between policies, controls, and regulatory (RSM) objects?** — and is that
   linkage consistent enough across markets to build one pipeline against?
3. **What structural nuances differ between the Global table and the country tables?** — since a
   naive "same schema everywhere" assumption would silently break on several markets.

The short answer to all three is that **the Global and US tables behave very differently from the
other 22**, and a single extraction strategy applied uniformly across all tables would silently
produce empty or degraded output for most markets.

## 2. Methodology

For each table:

1. Queried the full table (`policy_core_id`, `version_num`, `json_data`, `snapshot_time`,
   `transfer_date`).
2. Parsed `json_data` (a JSON string) into a dict and flattened nested paths.
3. **Selected the current state per policy** by taking the max `version_num` per `policy_core_id`.
   All text/linkage analysis below is on this current-version slice, not the full version history.
4. Extracted text from three distinct `sgmntText` locations:
   - `policyText.sgmntText` — the top-level obligation statement
   - `policyPurpose.sgmntText` — scope/risk-lineage narrative
   - `policyApplication.segment[].sgmntText` — one per segment, the detailed body content
5. Stripped HTML markup before measuring lengths (all three fields are HTML-formatted).
6. Bucketed character lengths: `empty` / `very_short (<100)` / `short (100–500)` /
   `fair (500–2000)` / `long (>2000)`.
7. Detected linkage two independent ways, since the schema is not consistent:
   - directly on the segment via `sgmntTypeCde` (e.g. `L1C-00000937`)
   - via the nested `objRel[]` list, checking `plcyObjTypeCde`, `cntlId`, `plcyObjId`

**Caveat on the "has text" check.** Text presence was tested as `length > 0` after HTML stripping.
This turns out to be too permissive — see [Finding 4.3](#43-whitespace-only-segment-text-inflates-completeness-metrics).

## 3. Population & Structure

### 3.1 The population is extremely concentrated

| Tier | Tables | Distinct Policies | Share of Total |
|---|---|---:|---:|
| Dominant | Global | 258 | ~64% |
| Substantial | US (26), UAE (24) | 50 | ~12% |
| Moderate | Australia (11), Bahrain (11), Qatar (10), China (7), Singapore (7), Hong Kong (6) | 52 | ~13% |
| Marginal | Algeria (9), Taiwan (5), South Africa (5), Indonesia (5), UK (5), India (4), Korea (3) | 36 | ~9% |
| Minimal (2 policies each) | Bangladesh, Brazil, Japan, New Zealand, Philippines, Thailand, Vietnam | 14 | ~3% |

**11 of the 24 tables hold 5 or fewer policies. Seven hold exactly two.** The Global table alone
holds roughly two-thirds of the entire policy population across all markets.

### 3.2 Six country tables share an identical statistical fingerprint

This is the most striking structural finding in the run and warrants direct SME confirmation.

**Bangladesh, Japan, New Zealand, Philippines, Thailand, and Vietnam** each report *exactly* the
same profile:

| Metric | Value (identical across all six) |
|---|---:|
| Total rows | 2 |
| Distinct policies | 2 |
| Max version seen | 2 |
| Avg versions per policy | 1.00 |
| `policyText` median chars | 529 |
| Total segments | 23 |
| Segment text median chars | 250 |
| Segment buckets | very_short 11, fair 6, short 4, long 2 |
| Segments with `objRel` | 0.0% |
| RSM / control linkage | none |

The probability of six independently-maintained country policy sets coinciding on every one of
these metrics — down to the identical segment-length bucket distribution — is effectively nil.
**The most likely explanation is that the same two source policies have been replicated into each
of these six country tables**, either as a template/default load or because these markets adopt
Global policies verbatim without local amendment.

This matters directly for linkage: if these are the same two policies six times over, deduplication
is needed before embedding, or the vector store will contain six near-identical copies competing
for the same matches.

### 3.3 Versioning behaviour splits sharply by table size

| Pattern | Tables | Interpretation |
|---|---|---|
| **Active revision** | UK (avg 2.80), US (2.46), Global (2.40), India (2.00), Australia (1.91), Singapore (1.86) | Policies genuinely evolve; version selection logic matters |
| **Largely static** | Qatar, China, Hong Kong, Indonesia, South Africa, and all seven 2-policy tables (all avg 1.00) | Every policy sits at a single version; version filtering is a no-op |

Two specific anomalies:

- **Global `max_version_seen = 71`** against an average of 2.40. A single policy has been revised
  71 times while the typical policy sits at 2. This is a sizable outlier worth identifying —
  it may be a genuinely volatile policy, or an artefact of system-generated administrative
  re-versioning (the earlier single-table EDA found `ADMINCHG` as a very common creation reason).
- **UK has the highest average version count (2.80) on only 5 policies** — 4 of its 5 policies are
  multi-version, giving it the most revision-intensive profile per policy of any market.

### 3.4 Table is live and growing

The Global table returned **620 rows** in this run. Earlier EDA passes on the same table (25 Aug)
returned 591, then 585, then 584 across consecutive queries in a single session. The table is being
actively appended to. **Any extract feeding a downstream index must be snapshot-pinned**, or
row counts and content will drift between the build and any subsequent validation.

### 3.5 JSON parse health is uniformly perfect

Every successfully-queried table returned **100.0% JSON parse success**. Whatever structural
problems exist in this data, malformed JSON is not among them. Parse failures can be ruled out as
an explanation for any of the content gaps below.

## 4. Text Content Findings

### 4.1 `policyText` is present nearly everywhere, but is often short

`policyText.sgmntText` is present for 100% of current-version rows in **every table except
Australia (90.91%)** — one Australian policy of eleven has no top-level policy text at all.

However, presence is not the same as substance. Median `policyText` length by table:

| Verdict | Median chars | Tables |
|---|---|---|
| **Fair/Good** (>1000) | 1271 / 1247 / 1216 / 1030 | US, UAE, South Africa, Algeria |
| **Moderate** (500–1000) | 944 / 856 / 773 / 529 | Qatar, Bahrain, Korea, and the six identical-fingerprint tables |
| **Short** (150–500) | 397 / 377 / 305 / 283 / 279 / 227 / 194 / 182 | Singapore, **Global**, India, Australia & Indonesia, UK, Hong Kong, China & Taiwan, Brazil |

**The Global table's `policyText` median is only 377 characters** — roughly a short paragraph —
despite holding 64% of all policies. Its distribution is 151 short / 81 fair / 17 long / 9
very_short. The top-level `policyText` field in GPPS appears to be a *summary statement* rather
than the full policy body; the substantive content lives in the segments.

### 4.2 Segment text is where the real content is — and it varies enormously

Segment-level text tells a very different story from `policyText`:

| Table | Segment median | Total segments | Segments/policy | Read |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
| UK | 1899 | 172 | 34.4 | Richest segment content of any table |
| Global | 1182 | 8,661 | 33.6 | Deep and voluminous |
| UAE | 712 | 422 | 17.6 | Solid |
| US | 507 | 1,007 | 38.7 | Solid, highest segment count per policy |
| South Africa / Japan / Korea / NZ / Philippines / Thailand / Vietnam / Bangladesh | 250–251 | 23–71 | 11.5–14.2 | Thin but non-trivial |
| Qatar / Singapore / Bahrain / Algeria / India | 188–223 | 50–157 | 12.5–14.0 | Thin |
| Australia | 114 | 197 | 17.9 | Very thin |
| **Brazil / China / Hong Kong / Indonesia / Taiwan** | **1** | 20–79 | 10–11.5 | **Degenerate — see 4.3** |

Note the inversion at the top: **the UK has the shortest `policyText` median (279) but the longest
segment median (1899)**. Relying on `policyText` alone would badly misjudge the UK's content
richness. This is the clearest evidence that `segment_text` — not `policyText` — should be the
primary text field for embedding.

### 4.3 Whitespace-only segment text inflates completeness metrics

Five tables — **Brazil, China, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Taiwan** — report `100.0% segments with text`
while simultaneously reporting a segment-text **median of 1 character**.

These two statements are only reconcilable if the majority of segments contain a single
non-empty-but-meaningless character (a space, `&nbsp;`, a stray punctuation mark) that passes a
`length > 0` check while carrying zero semantic content.

Supporting evidence from the bucket distributions:

| Table | very_short (<100) | short | fair | long | Total |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| China | 45 | 14 | 8 | 12 | 79 |
| Taiwan | 32 | 10 | 6 | 2 | 50 |
| Indonesia | 32 | 10 | 6 | 2 | 50 |
| Hong Kong | 40 | 12 | 9 | 8 | 69 |
| Brazil | 16 | 4 | 0 | 0 | 20 |

In every case the `very_short` bucket dominates (57–80% of segments), which is consistent with the
median-of-1 reading. Notably, these tables *do* still contain some genuinely long segments (China
has 12 `long` segments, Hong Kong 8) — so the content isn't uniformly absent, it's **sparse and
bimodal**: a few real segments surrounded by many placeholder ones.

**Consequence:** the headline "100% of segments have text" metric is not trustworthy as a data
quality signal. The completeness check must be re-run with a minimum meaningful-length threshold
(e.g. ≥20 characters after HTML stripping and whitespace normalisation).

### 4.4 Segment-level completeness is otherwise healthy

Setting aside the whitespace issue, **no policy in any successfully-queried table has more than 80%
of its segments missing text**. The Global table shows just 5 genuinely empty segments out of
8,661 (99.94% populated). Where segments exist, they are generally populated.

## 5. Linkage Findings

### 5.1 RSM (regulatory) linkage exists in exactly one table

This is the single most consequential finding for the linkage pipeline.

Across all 24 tables, RSM/regulatory objects (`objRel` entries with `plcyObjTypeCde = 'REG'`) appear
in **only `us_hsbc_gpps_gpps_policy`** — 346 segments spanning 19 of its 26 policies.

**Every other table returns zero.** This includes the Global table, which has 8,661 segments and
3,419 `CNTRL` objRel entries, but not a single `REG` entry.

| Table | Segments with objRel | objRel type breakdown | RSM segments |
|---|---:|---|---:|
| **US** | **62.76%** | None 375, **REG 346**, CNTRL 216, PLCY 70 | **346** |
| Global | 39.48% | None 5242, CNTRL 3419 | 0 |
| South Africa | 30.99% | None 49, CNTRL 13, PLCY 9 | 0 |
| UK | 29.65% | None 121, CNTRL 33, PLCY 18 | 0 |
| Australia | 29.44% | None 139, CNTRL 35, PLCY 23 | 0 |
| UAE | 27.49% | None 306, CNTRL 70, PLCY 46 | 0 |
| Bahrain | 27.39% | None 114, CNTRL 27, PLCY 16 | 0 |
| Qatar | 26.43% | None 103, CNTRL 23, PLCY 14 | 0 |
| Algeria | 25.78% | None 95, CNTRL 22, PLCY 11 | 0 |
| India | 12.00% | None 44, CNTRL 3, PLCY 3 | 0 |
| China | 7.59% | None 73, CNTRL 3, PLCY 3 | 0 |
| Bangladesh, Brazil, HK, Indonesia, Japan, Korea, NZ, Philippines, Singapore, Taiwan, Thailand, Vietnam | 0.00% | None only | 0 |

The US table is also the only one where a *majority* of segments (62.76%) carry any `objRel` at
all — every other table sits at 31% or below.

**Two readings are possible and the SME must distinguish them:** either RSM tagging is a US-specific
regulatory practice (plausible — US regulatory obligations are unusually granular and explicitly
enumerated), or RSM linkage is a newer capability that has only been backfilled for the US so far
and will eventually propagate. These imply very different pipeline designs.

### 5.2 The Global table uses a different control-identification mechanism entirely

Control codes appear in two structurally different places depending on the table:

- **Global table:** `sgmntTypeCde` on the segment itself carries the L1 control code directly
  (5,120 of 8,661 segments = 59%; the remaining 3,541 are `None`).
- **All 23 country tables:** `sgmntTypeCde` is `None` for **100% of segments**, without exception.
  Where controls exist, they are reachable only via the nested `objRel[]` list with
  `plcyObjTypeCde = 'CNTRL'`.

This means **code written against the Global table's schema will silently find zero controls in
every country table, and vice versa**. Any extraction logic must check both paths. (The EDA script
was built to do exactly this, which is why control linkage was detected in country tables at all.)

### 5.3 Control linkage coverage splits the estate roughly in half

| Has control linkage (11 tables) | Control segments (policies covered) |
|---|---|
| Global | 5,759 (245 of 258 policies) |
| US | 153 (21 of 26) |
| UAE | 83 (14 of 24) |
| Bahrain | 36 (9 of 11) |
| UK | 33 (4 of 5) |
| Algeria | 29 (7 of 9) |
| Qatar | 29 (8 of 10) |
| Australia | 26 (4 of 11) |
| South Africa | 9 (2 of 5) |
| China | 6 (1 of 7) |
| India | 6 (1 of 4) |

| No control linkage at all (12 tables) |
|---|
| Bangladesh, Brazil, Hong Kong, Indonesia, Japan, Korea, New Zealand, Philippines, Singapore, Taiwan, Thailand, Vietnam |

Coverage *within* the linked tables also varies widely: Global covers 95% of its policies (245/258),
but Australia covers only 36% (4/11) and India only 25% (1/4).

**Singapore is a notable outlier in this list.** It has 7 policies, 72 segments, a healthy segment
median of 217 characters, and 5 of its 7 policies are multi-version — so it is clearly an actively
maintained market. Yet it has **0.0% segments with `objRel`** and no linkage whatsoever. Unlike the
2-policy minimal tables, Singapore's absence of linkage cannot be explained by the market simply
being unpopulated.

### 5.4 A third linkage type (`PLCY`) exists and has not yet been assessed

Nine tables contain `objRel` entries with `plcyObjTypeCde = 'PLCY'` — UAE (46), Australia (23),
Bahrain (16), UK (18), Qatar (14), Algeria (11), South Africa (9), India (3), China (3), plus US (70).

This appears to be a **policy-to-policy relationship** (cross-reference, parent/child, or
supersession). It was not part of the original analysis scope and has not been characterised. If it
encodes policy hierarchy, it may be directly useful for linkage — e.g. propagating a match from a
parent policy to its children, or avoiding double-counting related policies in results.

